7. Root Cause Analysis

Likely contributing factors:

Compromised credentials for newuser

Remote access enabled without strong authentication controls

Unmanaged Linux device with weaker monitoring and enforcement

Lack of enforced MFA

The unmanaged status of the endpoint significantly increased risk exposure.

8. Containment & Remediation Actions

Immediate isolation of MTS-Web from the network

Credential reset for affected accounts

Firewall block of IP 164.92.218.170

Full malware scan and forensic analysis

Reimage/rebuild of endpoint due to confirmed privilege escalation

Enforcement of MFA and stronger authentication policies

Restriction of remote access for unmanaged endpoints

Enhanced logging for script execution and writable directory activity

9. Impact Assessment

Confirmed system compromise

Elevated privileges obtained by attacker

Malware executed on endpoint

Potential risk of lateral movement (no evidence observed but possible)

Given the multi-stage nature of the attack, full reimaging was the safest remediation path.

10. Lessons Learned

Valid credentials significantly reduce detection friction for attackers

Unmanaged endpoints present disproportionate risk

World-writable directory monitoring is critical in Linux environments

Log correlation is essential to identifying multi-stage attack chains

Analyst Reflection

This investigation reinforced the importance of correlating authentication logs, process execution data, and malware alerts to identify structured adversary behavior rather than treating alerts as isolated events.
